{"title":"2022 AIME II Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Coord_bash)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Coord bash)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Power_of_a_Point)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Power of a Point)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Mathematical_Dexterity)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Mathematical Dexterity)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Power_of_Logic\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Power of Logic</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A right square pyramid with volume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/c/b1cb5bdce2d06d2419066a2b55ab01d824217bd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$54$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> has a base with side length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/e/1de3ed5fb7fa9b1fa73395d65c43e5bc81cd9d61.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6.$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" /> The five vertices of the pyramid all lie on a sphere with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/9/9b9ffa9646e74f6e94a4a66b824c3a3f08f4f4b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac mn$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"18\" height=\"33\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/d/02dbab3601fa6cce490d54263017048f69e1a35d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/4/c64a045f830f45315105799c102d69a5e288a845.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import three; import settings; leftbutton=new string[] {&quot;rotate&quot;,&quot;zoom&quot;,&quot;shift&quot;,&quot;pan&quot;}; middlebutton=new string[] {&quot;&quot;}; rightbutton=new string[] {&quot;zoom&quot;,&quot;rotateX&quot;,&quot;rotateY&quot;,&quot;rotateZ&quot;}; wheelup=new string[] {&quot;zoomin&quot;}; wheeldown=new string[] {&quot;zoomout&quot;}; import three; pen p = rgb(&quot;00FF80&quot;)+opacity(0.25); pen q = rgb(&quot;FF8000&quot;)+opacity(0.75); triple A = (-3,-3,0); triple B = (-3,3,0); triple C = (3,3,0); triple D = (3,-3,0); triple F = (0,0,9/2); surface sphere = shift((0,0,1/4))*scale3(17/4)*unitsphere; draw(sphere,p); draw(A--B--C--D--A--F--B--F--C--F--D--F--A,q); dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D); dot(F); [/asy]\" width=\"248\" height=\"252\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2022_AIME_II_3.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/bd/2022_AIME_II_3.png/300px-2022_AIME_II_3.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"300\" height=\"324\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/b/bd/2022_AIME_II_3.png/450px-2022_AIME_II_3.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/b/bd/2022_AIME_II_3.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Although I can't draw the exact picture of this problem, but it is quite easy to imagine that four vertices of the base of this pyramid is on a circle (Radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/c/e0ceda310cd808afdeac002f0a461d31b5c5ecbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{2}} = 3\\sqrt{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"86\" height=\"42\" />). Since all five vertices are on the sphere, the distances of the spherical center and the vertices are the same: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/2/9b25f8e64b484493fda944d25cad453423041fe6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l$\" width=\"5\" height=\"12\" />. Because of the symmetrical property of the pyramid,\nwe can imagine that the line of the apex and the (sphere's) center will intersect the square at the (base's) center.\n</p><p>Since the volume is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/a/c9a0460c356977019d9846311354468d5bcbe184.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$54 = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot S \\cdot h = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 6^2 \\cdot h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"197\" height=\"38\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/a/5ea43a5851f45e3383ac5d032d38da9af65c292b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$h=\\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"50\" height=\"38\" /> is the height of this pyramid, we have: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/f/d5f7651408b3554c586998cd039edb557bdc9d22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l^2=\\left(\\frac{9}{2}-l\\right)^2+\\left(3\\sqrt{2}\\right)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"198\" height=\"48\" /> according to the Pythagorean theorem.\n</p><p>Solve this equation will give us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/7/e3767d074c4b63e3016f2c8f8eca92950142cb72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$l = \\frac{17}{4},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"59\" height=\"38\" /> therefore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/7/04704b22758b88646653c64ff0586fbcdbac3748.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m+n=\\boxed{021}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"119\" height=\"25\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To start, we find the height of the pyramid. By the volume of a pyramid formula, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/41280dba61c5cb86dd0b5ab245c4b0571d1c69b5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac13 \\cdot 6^2 \\cdot h=54 \\implies h=\\frac92.\\]\" width=\"207\" height=\"37\" /> Next, let us find the length of the non-base sides of the pyramid. By the Pythagorean Theorem, noting that the distance from one vertex of the base to the center of the base is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/b/77b82eaeb10f04d75b3960ffb259dce125832dba.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac12 \\cdot 6\\sqrt2=3\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"119\" height=\"38\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/6/7568a8673657eb8699e69661e8c82e765d9b2933.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac92\\right)^2+(3\\sqrt2)^2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{153}4}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{17}}2.\\]\" width=\"323\" height=\"53\" /> Taking the cross section of the pyramid and transforming the problem into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-d, it suffices to find the radius of the circumcircle of a triangle of side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/8/1a8414f8b369db4a6f7dedaa8157d87049782255.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3\\sqrt{17}}2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"43\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/8/1a8414f8b369db4a6f7dedaa8157d87049782255.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3\\sqrt{17}}2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"48\" height=\"43\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc83f571fe800ecc5672659b4391c4005a0b7a06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6\\sqrt2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" />. This turns out to be easy by the formula <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/d/bbde5d6a10f64624a7e76405e2ea632e4ecf1092.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{abc}{4A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"65\" height=\"38\" />, and through computing this value (the work has been left out) we find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/d/e1dc3d41dfa360c49d0c29016e54ff33d37ea313.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{17}4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"62\" height=\"38\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/d/e3d67ffc6d5f037a75a675507a7cf3ad9872ea3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{021}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"42\" height=\"24\" />.\n</p><p>~A1001\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Coord_bash.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Coord_bash)\">Solution 3 (Coord bash)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Coord bash)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By the volume of a pyramid formula, we have that the height of the pyramid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adcd622a1ba9d557e1e14b7810cd5852e5ec05e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{9}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" />. Since the base is a square with side length 6, the simplest way to place it in the coordinate plane is to put the center of the square at the origin and let the base be on the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/7/3f79c3676b62fb47243e0d357c87115b6f3395e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$xy$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"13\" /> plane. Then, the vertices of the base would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/b/eeba04b96983a9355e2545bdcb16bb10c55e56ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,3,0), (3,-3,0), (-3,3,0), (-3,-3,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"313\" height=\"20\" /> in some order. Also, let the vertex be  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/8337f84f92454e35783480f343d6fd195594b830.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,\\frac{9}{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"60\" height=\"37\" />. Recall that the formula for a sphere is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/3/ee37afd79752e0b5f673e7ed6be8478a0b21fdfa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(x-a)^2+(y-b)^2+(z-c)^2=r^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"273\" height=\"21\" /> where the center is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/d/f3d9e22097e20a1249b5332202b24941a4bf9e1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a,b,c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"57\" height=\"20\" /> and the radius is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Symmetry gives that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/6/1660d43b4960efd50409826b189fce815544ca22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b=0$\" width=\"74\" height=\"12\" />. Plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/6/cd65416b65c1e7130644be547c143d48a2b1edb6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3,3,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/8337f84f92454e35783480f343d6fd195594b830.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0,\\frac{9}{2})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"60\" height=\"37\" /> and you get the system of equation \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05ec35e7a80931cd011245f36f5aaccef0b1fb42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$18+c^2=r^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"98\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d32cd5f846d781b82db92ad03389c574baf39570.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(\\frac{9}{2}-c)^2=r^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Solving gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/532424b6abfdd3a6077133969f240b680aef963d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c=1/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"61\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/b/95b2d0c88883063f644bdbcfcf6d554d73c633da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=17/4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"20\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/64721a1d4d4a7f837ed8e9a4aa77f4f7eadcbcd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17+4=\\boxed{021}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"114\" height=\"25\" />.~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Ddk001\" title=\"Ddk001\">Ddk001</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Power_of_a_Point.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Power_of_a_Point)\">Solution 4 (Power of a Point)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Power of a Point)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the volume of a square pyramid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/8/7d8de84bbe25f038280874e5d07ec3d5731101c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot{s^2}\\cdot{h}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" />. The volume of the pyramid is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/c/b1cb5bdce2d06d2419066a2b55ab01d824217bd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$54$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> and the square's side length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Plugging the information back into the formula, we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/2/f82b823344455dfcb2126de29aa787b792d1edeb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 6^2 \\cdot h=54 \\implies h=\\frac{9}{2}\\]\" width=\"202\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p>By Pythagorean theorem, we can say the diagonal of the square is \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/f/51f8d8134c9f3d5932764b74b79f923c48f0d345.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sqrt{6^2+6^2}=6\\sqrt{2}\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"18\" />. \n</p><p>If we draw a line going through the poles of the sphere, we can see that the line perpendicularly bisects the square's diagonal. If we take the cross-section of the sphere making the problem <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-d, and call the radius of the sphere <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, by the power of point, we can say\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/7/9c7041afe5e1d39cb16f0c0f356a34c5b4c94e03.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot(2r-\\frac{9}{2})=3\\sqrt2\\cdot{3\\sqrt2}\\]\" width=\"196\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>solving this equation gives you\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/2/0b27463f78c17f5402d2eb6be0cb3ef0d123692d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r=\\frac{17}{4}\\]\" width=\"53\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/7/64721a1d4d4a7f837ed8e9a4aa77f4f7eadcbcd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17+4=\\boxed{021}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"114\" height=\"25\" />. ~<a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=User:Lentarot\" title=\"User:Lentarot\">User:Lentarot</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_.28Mathematical_Dexterity.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_(Mathematical_Dexterity)\">Video Solution (Mathematical Dexterity)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution (Mathematical Dexterity)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UJAYW6YNFVU\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UJAYW6YNFVU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Power_of_Logic\">Video Solution by Power of Logic</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Power of Logic\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/YieIiYCDytM\">https://youtu.be/YieIiYCDytM</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II\" title=\"2022 AIME II\">2022 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2022_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2022 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a